WEBVTT

00:04.040 --> 00:09.480
And now in the next section, I'm going to show you the best practices on how to create tables.

00:09.480 --> 00:13.280
So the best practices of DDL data definition language.

00:13.280 --> 00:18.960
If you have a poor definition of your tables, this has a great impact on the performance of your queries.

00:19.000 --> 00:19.320
All right.

00:19.320 --> 00:23.400
So now we have here like a DDL in order to create a table customer info.

00:23.520 --> 00:26.080
And it is not really following best practices.

00:26.080 --> 00:27.800
So let's go through it one by one.

00:27.840 --> 00:32.560
The first tip is try to avoid the data types varchar and text.

00:32.560 --> 00:33.720
If it's possible.

00:33.720 --> 00:34.920
The Varchar and text.

00:34.960 --> 00:40.600
They are like one of the worst data types for performance because they consume a lot of resources.

00:40.600 --> 00:41.760
Whatever you do like.

00:41.760 --> 00:46.720
For example, if you are sorting the data by a column that is varchar or text, it is very expensive

00:46.760 --> 00:47.400
operation.

00:47.400 --> 00:52.120
The same thing if you go like create an index on top of such a column, it's going to be as well expensive.

00:52.120 --> 00:56.120
And they cause a lot of problems with the data fragmentations and many issues.

00:56.120 --> 01:00.400
So try as much as you can to skip those data type if it's possible.

01:00.400 --> 01:04.840
So now let's go and review all those columns in order to see whether we can change something about it,

01:05.080 --> 01:06.890
because it has a lot of bar charts.

01:06.930 --> 01:10.250
So the first one over here, we have a bar chart because it is the first name.

01:10.250 --> 01:11.330
Well it is okay.

01:11.370 --> 01:12.450
Now moving on to the next one.

01:12.450 --> 01:17.530
We have the last name as a text, which is not really good because text is worse than varchar.

01:17.730 --> 01:20.010
So it's better to use Varchar than a text.

01:20.010 --> 01:21.130
So here we have to fix it.

01:21.130 --> 01:24.370
So Varchar and I'm going to go with the links 50.

01:24.410 --> 01:26.090
Now moving on to the countries.

01:26.090 --> 01:27.850
So the country is going to be Varchar.

01:28.010 --> 01:29.130
We cannot change that.

01:29.130 --> 01:30.570
It contain characters.

01:30.570 --> 01:33.090
So the next one is the score of the customer.

01:33.450 --> 01:37.170
Here we can do something about it because scores are only numbers.

01:37.170 --> 01:39.410
So that's why we can go and skip this one.

01:39.450 --> 01:43.130
So let's remove it and say you are integer.

01:43.130 --> 01:45.570
And with that we have avoided using the Varchar.

01:45.570 --> 01:47.450
And the same thing goes for the birthday.

01:47.490 --> 01:49.050
The birthday is a date.

01:49.050 --> 01:50.770
And here we have it as a varchar.

01:50.810 --> 01:52.010
Well this is not really good.

01:52.010 --> 01:55.490
And we can skip that by having this column as a date.

01:55.490 --> 01:58.410
So date is way better than having a varchar.

01:58.450 --> 01:58.890
All right.

01:58.890 --> 02:00.130
And the next one is integer.

02:00.130 --> 02:01.810
So with that we have fixed few stuff.

02:01.810 --> 02:04.370
So we have fixed the score and the birthday.

02:04.370 --> 02:06.330
And with that we have saved some storage.

02:06.450 --> 02:10.410
If we have an index on the score it's going to be way better than having a Varchar.

02:10.410 --> 02:14.190
And if you are filtering the data based on the birthday, it's going to be faster.

02:14.190 --> 02:17.310
So again, try your best to avoid the Varchar and the text.

02:17.350 --> 02:22.630
I've seen in many projects that a lot of developers tend to use the Varchar, and I understand it is

02:22.630 --> 02:27.750
easier to make everything as a Varchar than deciding whether it is an integer or date float, and so

02:27.750 --> 02:31.430
on, because you can fit everything in the varchar and text.

02:31.430 --> 02:32.910
But this is lazy.

02:33.190 --> 02:38.150
Take time to understand the content of this column and try to assign it to the correct data type, because

02:38.150 --> 02:40.270
this has really impact on the performance.

02:40.430 --> 02:45.630
Okay, to the next one it says avoid using max or overly large lengths.

02:45.630 --> 02:48.910
So now we have to keep our eyes on the links of each data type.

02:48.950 --> 02:55.430
Actually the Varchar not only it can waste like a lot of storage, it also can like mislead the SQL

02:55.710 --> 03:02.550
by creating large indexes, which is totally unnecessary because the data itself is small, but because

03:02.550 --> 03:07.630
you have defined like a large length, it's going to check those informations and make a decision to

03:07.670 --> 03:08.990
make a big index.

03:08.990 --> 03:13.830
And large indexes are always problematic because they're going to slow everything down by sorting the

03:13.830 --> 03:17.280
data, by retrieving data, by updating the index.

03:17.280 --> 03:24.920
So it is really bad practices if you go blindly and define everywhere max or 255, again, give it a

03:24.920 --> 03:28.360
chance to think about each column and predict a length for it.

03:28.400 --> 03:32.520
So for example, if you check over here we are saying first name varchar max.

03:32.520 --> 03:38.600
Well, most of the first names are short, so we don't need like the maximum size of a Varchar to fit

03:38.640 --> 03:39.480
a first name.

03:39.480 --> 03:43.280
So here we can go easily instead of max with the 50.

03:43.320 --> 03:45.400
And the same thing goes for the column country.

03:45.400 --> 03:49.760
We don't need 255 characters for the country name.

03:49.760 --> 03:52.920
We can go with something more realistic, like around 50.

03:52.960 --> 03:56.040
I think you can even go smaller, but it's fine to have 50.

03:56.080 --> 04:01.400
So the best practice here is to analyze your data and to predict the size of each column.

04:01.400 --> 04:04.280
And don't be lazy by just defining max everywhere.

04:04.320 --> 04:07.280
I know it's faster, but it's bad for performance.

04:07.320 --> 04:08.160
Okay, what do you have?

04:08.200 --> 04:13.040
Else use the constraint not null as much as possible then not.

04:13.040 --> 04:15.280
Null is amazing and has a lot of advantages.

04:15.280 --> 04:19.080
Of course, the biggest advantage is that the data integrity of your table.

04:19.080 --> 04:24.250
So with that you make sure no nulls are inserted in specific column, but it is as well.

04:24.290 --> 04:29.290
Good practices to use it for improving the performance, because if you are creating an index, you're

04:29.290 --> 04:30.650
going to get a better index.

04:30.650 --> 04:31.490
Performance.

04:31.530 --> 04:37.050
Since SQL knows there is no nulls inside my tree, inside the index, and in the other side.

04:37.050 --> 04:42.570
If you are writing a query, we tend to use a filter where we say specific column should not be null.

04:42.610 --> 04:47.210
But if you make sure that in the DDL it is not null, then you can skip this filter.

04:47.210 --> 04:49.490
And with that you are reducing the size of your query.

04:49.490 --> 04:53.130
So what we're going to do, we're going to go through all the columns and decide whether it is not null

04:53.130 --> 04:53.770
and null.

04:53.770 --> 04:56.810
So for example the first name and the last name they should not be null.

04:56.810 --> 04:58.930
So that's why I'm going to say not null.

04:58.970 --> 05:03.770
And the same thing for the last name, not null for the customer ID we're going to talk about it soon

05:03.770 --> 05:05.490
because we're going to convert it to a primary key.

05:05.530 --> 05:07.330
And primary keys are usually not null.

05:07.330 --> 05:11.530
So now for the country, we may have it in the business that it should not be null.

05:11.530 --> 05:13.930
So we go and make a constraint about it.

05:13.970 --> 05:16.250
Now about the total purchases and scores.

05:16.290 --> 05:19.810
If it is a new customer, maybe we can have a null inside our data.

05:19.810 --> 05:20.810
So we're going to leave it empty.

05:20.810 --> 05:23.210
And I think birthday is going to be usually optional.

05:23.210 --> 05:24.770
So we're going to leave it as well.

05:24.810 --> 05:27.460
And whether the customer is employee or not.

05:27.500 --> 05:29.380
This could be as well a null.

05:29.420 --> 05:33.820
So with that we have found out like three columns where we can have a constraint about the not null.

05:33.820 --> 05:38.540
And if we go and create like an index on the country, it's going to be a better index.

05:38.580 --> 05:38.820
Okay.

05:38.860 --> 05:44.900
Moving on to the next one, it says make sure that all your tables inside the database have a clustered

05:44.900 --> 05:45.780
primary key.

05:45.860 --> 05:49.900
And as well it's going to help you building the relationship between tables where you have primary keys

05:49.900 --> 05:50.740
and foreign keys.

05:50.780 --> 05:53.100
And you can join tables then very easily.

05:53.100 --> 05:56.220
And as well a primary key has importance for the performance.

05:56.220 --> 06:01.460
And in SQL server the default is going to be a clustered index, which is really good to have an index

06:01.460 --> 06:07.140
on the primary key, because sometimes you are doing like an update operations or delete operations.

06:07.180 --> 06:10.340
It can help out by the lookups of joining tables.

06:10.340 --> 06:13.500
So there are a lot of performance benefits of having a primary key.

06:13.500 --> 06:16.540
And make sure that all your tables having a primary key.

06:16.540 --> 06:19.140
So as you can see the issue of our table, we don't have a primary key.

06:19.140 --> 06:21.500
And our primary key can be that customer ID.

06:21.500 --> 06:24.460
So let's go and do that primary key.

06:24.700 --> 06:26.860
And as I said as a default can be clustered.

06:26.860 --> 06:31.400
But I'm going to write it down in case if you are working with different databases.

06:31.400 --> 06:33.200
Make sure it is clustered.

06:33.240 --> 06:33.440
Okay.

06:33.480 --> 06:34.520
Moving on to the next one.

06:34.520 --> 06:36.280
It's not only about the primary key.

06:36.320 --> 06:38.400
We have to take care of our foreign keys.

06:38.400 --> 06:43.200
So the best practice says create non-clustered index for the foreign keys.

06:43.480 --> 06:44.960
If they are frequently used.

06:44.960 --> 06:49.560
The foreign keys are usually important in order to connect and join two tables.

06:49.560 --> 06:51.600
And usually we frequently use it.

06:51.600 --> 06:55.320
And not only that, we use it sometimes in order to filter the data.

06:55.320 --> 06:59.120
And if we create a non-clustered index for that, it can improve the speed.

06:59.120 --> 07:00.520
So what we can do, it's very simple.

07:00.520 --> 07:04.320
We're going to go and create a non-clustered index on our table.

07:04.320 --> 07:07.800
Customers info for the foreign key employee ID.

07:07.800 --> 07:08.480
So how to do it.

07:08.480 --> 07:09.080
It's very simple.

07:09.080 --> 07:12.680
We're going to go and say create non-clustered index on our table.

07:12.680 --> 07:16.000
The customer's info on our foreign key the employee ID.

07:16.000 --> 07:21.600
But again make sure that this is an important foreign key that is used frequently from your queries.

07:21.640 --> 07:26.640
All right friends, so as you can see, there are a lot of best practices on how to improve and optimize

07:26.640 --> 07:27.440
the DDL.

07:27.520 --> 07:31.160
Having a healthy DDL can improve the performance of your queries.

07:31.200 --> 07:36.000
Now in the next section, I'm going to show you the best practices and tips and tricks about indexing.

07:36.120 --> 07:36.760
So let's go.
